WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.290
MALLABY: So let's start off with the baseline.

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:12.050
We have just had, Nouriel, the latest version
of the World Economic Update from the IMF.

00:00:12.050 --> 00:00:14.599
Yet again, there's a growth downgrade.

00:00:14.599 --> 00:00:16.840
Do these people ever upgrade?

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:17.840
What's going on?

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.840
(Laughter.)

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.840
What do you think, Nouriel?

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:25.680
Give us-give us your reaction to the 3.2 percent
growth projection for this year.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:31.200
ROUBINI: Well, official institutions usually
always are slightly more optimistic than the

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.200
outcomes.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:36.380
And, well, even the IMF knows about, in this
world, of low economic growth.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:41.079
Me and my colleagues at Roubini call it the
new abnormal, and the Chinese call it the

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:42.079
new normal.

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:44.480
The IMF calls it the new mediocre.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.340
Larry Summers speaks about a cycle of stagnation.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.790
Ray Dalio speaks about the great deleveraging.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:56.860
I would say, regardless of the label, I think
there is a broader, now, consensus that potential

00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:03.510
growth, both in advanced economies and emerging
markets, has fallen, is in part the consequence

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:09.040
of the global financial crisis, aging of population-not
just in advanced economies, but also important

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.060
emerging markets like China, Russia, Korea.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:16.450
After the global financial crisis, investment
collapsed, and it certainly has not recovered.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:20.970
So if human and physical capital are not growing
as fast as before, potential growth is lower,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:22.860
to increasingly need structural reforms.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:27.119
But structural reforms are hard to make, both
in advanced economies, in emerging market,

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:30.100
in democracies, in less-democratic countries.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:37.200
We also have probably an impact of-(inaudible)-probably
might have implied some slowdown or reduction

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:43.369
in trend growth, rising inequality that distribute
income from labor to capital, from those who

00:01:43.369 --> 00:01:45.220
spend to those who save.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:50.420
So we have not just a global savings glut,
but we also have a global investment slump.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:54.650
And this is a world in which potential growth
is going to stay stagnant.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:59.760
And as there is deleveraging-first in the
U.S., then Europe, now in emerging markets-actual

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.730
growth goes below potential for a while.

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.820
And the third leg of this great deleveraging
is now in emerging markets.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.649
Maybe the U.S. is closer to having done some
of that deleveraging and closer to achieving

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:14.140
potential growth, but even the U.S. potential
growth is, at the best, maybe 2 percent.

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:17.610
So that's the new abnormal or new mediocre,
whatever you want to call it.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:23.870
MALLABY: So, John, we're talking about a situation
in which, for a variety of reasons, post-crisis

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:33.340
growth is slower; less investment; some redistribution,
as Nouriel says, of money from those who spend

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:38.980
it to those who save it-he didn't mention
technology, which of course has big distributional

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:46.190
impacts-all of which leads to a political
reaction, which we're seeing a bit worldwide.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:51.530
Can you-do you think there's a bit of a negative
feedback loop here-lower growth leads to not

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:55.239
only secular stagnation, but sort of secular
policy stagnation?

00:02:55.239 --> 00:03:00.180
LIPSKY: No, do you mean in terms of the IMF
or the consensus forecast, or in reality?

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:04.350
MALLABY: No, I mean, I was thinking more in
reality-(laughter)-and in sort of a feedback

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:10.159
loop between, you know, disappointing economics
leads to sort of toxic politics leads to more

00:03:10.159 --> 00:03:11.159
disappointing economics.

00:03:11.159 --> 00:03:13.090
LIPSKY: Yeah, for sure.

00:03:13.090 --> 00:03:19.769
But I would say, when you read the IMF-the
WEO, the World Economic Outlook, it says we

00:03:19.769 --> 00:03:25.090
both mark down the forecast, and the risks
are skewed to the downside.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:28.680
Which says-to an economist, it says, well,
that means it's not a fair forecast because

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:30.840
you're supposed to have the risk balanced.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:37.510
So I would like to say that the recent downgrade
may be-in growth may be fine, but I'm not

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:42.340
completely convinced that the risks are only
on the downside from that forecast.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:47.360
But the feedback loop that you talk about
is evident.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:48.990
We can see it in politics here.

00:03:48.990 --> 00:03:54.400
We can see it in Europe, we can see it in
Japan, and elsewhere that this-a sense of

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.110
dissatisfaction that produces pressure on
authorities to do things, and to do things

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:05.080
that have been different than what they've
done before, which may not always be productive

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.420
and with good results.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:15.480
MALLABY: So, Willem, people in this post-2008
negative equilibrium have turned most aggressively

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.090
to central banks.

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:20.509
You know a lot about central banks.

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:28.310
Their latest iteration of this kind of rabbit-out-of-the-hat
was to experiment with negative interest rates.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.120
That didn't go so well in Japan.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.871
Talk a bit about that, whether negative interest
rates can be salvaged as a policy tool, and

00:04:37.871 --> 00:04:40.320
if not, what next.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.150
BUITER: In principle, negative interest rates
are a totally conventional instrument, right?

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.360
It simply means lower interest rates-(laughs)-than
when they were positive.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.610
And because an interest rate on the whole
tends to stimulate demand, whether it is in

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:59.120
positive ranges or negative ranges, two conditions
it doesn't: that's either the policy rate

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:03.840
cut or monetary policy action in general has
no effect on asset prices and yields-in sort

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:10.000
of textbook that's horizontal LM, liquidity
trap; or, yes, we move asset prices and yields

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.050
with the policy, but there's no transmission
to the real economy.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:14.759
And I think we're very close to that.

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:19.350
And the main reason for that is because of
leverage that every country in the world except

00:05:19.350 --> 00:05:22.490
North Korea is suffering from.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:23.490
(Laughter.)

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:26.630
So-and probably they as well, but yeah.

00:05:26.630 --> 00:05:32.470
And there's a special handicap to negative
rates, is that they can't become very negative

00:05:32.470 --> 00:05:34.660
because there's the zero lower bound, right?

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:39.389
The carry cost of currency, basically, of
cash, means that you can't really, without

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:46.580
arm-twisting and moral suasion, put the policy
rate much below minus-50 basis points, half

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:47.580
a percent.

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:48.979
They're going to get close to that.

00:05:48.979 --> 00:05:56.440
I don't think that the fact that the day after
Japan cut its rates the yen appreciated means

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.680
that there is something perverse about changing
rates.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:05.720
Many other things happened, and this is not
a ceteris paribus sort of clean experiment

00:06:05.720 --> 00:06:07.760
of an unexpected cut in rates.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.150
So I have some hope.

00:06:11.150 --> 00:06:14.169
Going negative, I think, in this environment
does very little, right?

00:06:14.169 --> 00:06:19.680
It probably doesn't do, on balance, any harm,
but certainly it doesn't stimulate aggregate

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.680
demand.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.680
ROUBINI: Can I make a-
MALLABY: But let me push on that.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:23.569
I mean, surely on the banks it did do some
harm.

00:06:23.569 --> 00:06:24.569
BUITER: No, no, no.

00:06:24.569 --> 00:06:25.569
MALLABY: Bank profits got squeezed.

00:06:25.569 --> 00:06:26.569
Isn't that right?

00:06:26.569 --> 00:06:29.860
BUITER: No, banks live not off the level of
rates, they live off spreads.

00:06:29.860 --> 00:06:34.380
And so, if the rates can be negative but the
yield curve's upward-sloping, banks are happy.

00:06:34.380 --> 00:06:43.160
The one problem was that banks have so far,
largely for cultural reasons, viewed passing

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:50.479
on their negative deposit rate to the central
bank to retail depositors and other non-wholesale

00:06:50.479 --> 00:06:52.979
sorts of funders as a taboo, right?

00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:55.860
Well, that's their choice, right?

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.569
I think there is, again, a limit to what you
can do there.

00:06:58.569 --> 00:07:03.150
Technically, the effective lower bound is
either minus the carry cost of currency by

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:09.440
banks or minus the carry cost of currency
for households, plus the minimum margin between

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:12.980
the bank deposit rate at the central bank
and what they pay their depositors.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.760
So I don't think you can get much, without
arm-twisting, below minus-75.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:23.449
But I think there's hope that a few years
from now, for the next crisis, when we hopefully

00:07:23.449 --> 00:07:28.430
have less leverage and we need to use monetary
policy again, because we'll be at the zero

00:07:28.430 --> 00:07:32.270
lower bound on and off for years, possibly
decades to come, right?

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:36.419
I don't think that the neutral-the (risk-reveal
?) interest rate that balances full employment,

00:07:36.419 --> 00:07:39.740
saving, and investment-is going to be positive
for the foreseeable future.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:43.479
And it means that in any downturn you're going
to be against the zero lower bound again,

00:07:43.479 --> 00:07:46.150
and you really want to go to minus-2, minus-3.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:50.699
And there's a bit of luck: three years from
now, five years from now, the large-denomination

00:07:50.699 --> 00:07:55.200
dollar bill will be $10, right, which increases
the carry cost of currency.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:08:00.310
Getting rid of cash is one obvious way to
eliminate the zero lower bound.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:05.970
The ECB is leading the way for unrelated reasons,
having done away with the 500-euro note, right,

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:11.180
which is one of the tax-evader's dreams, of
course.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:13.160
The Swiss, we're still waiting to get rid
of the thousand-franc note.

00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:15.840
But-
MALLABY: So you think that the largest American

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.259
currency note should be $10?

00:08:17.259 --> 00:08:18.259
BUITER: Yes.

00:08:18.259 --> 00:08:21.220
MALLABY: Fifty shouldn't be allowed, 20-
BUITER: No, no, absolutely, yes.

00:08:21.220 --> 00:08:25.910
This is for those who are-
MALLABY: So the sheer threat of holding sweaty

00:08:25.910 --> 00:08:28.479
paper-
LIPSKY: For the convenience of central bankers-

00:08:28.479 --> 00:08:29.479
BUITER: Yes.

00:08:29.479 --> 00:08:30.830
LIPSKY: -I think.

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:31.830
(Laughter.)

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:36.750
BUITER: So central banks can set negative
interest rate as far as possible.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:40.469
And also, of course, which is-whenever I say
this, I get hate mail from libertarians like

00:08:40.469 --> 00:08:48.790
you wouldn't believe-to penalize the criminal
fraternity-you know, tax evasion, money laundering,

00:08:48.790 --> 00:08:52.430
terrorism financing, and all that-which is
a cash-based business.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:58.961
But of course the libertarian says that the
citizen needs anonymity given by cash as a

00:08:58.961 --> 00:09:02.410
defense against the predatory and encroaching
state.

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:04.901
LIPSKY: If I could-
MALLABY: So Willem is saying that the-that

00:09:04.901 --> 00:09:09.810
the only thing that's stopping us from this
negative rate working a bit better is if we

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.269
could just get our acts together and abolish
cash and break this taboo of that-

00:09:13.269 --> 00:09:17.779
ROUBINI: Well, even before we abolish cash
and eventually get there with digital money,

00:09:17.779 --> 00:09:22.130
I think that you have to prevent banks for
switching from excess reserves into cash.

00:09:22.130 --> 00:09:27.220
And I was just in Europe this past 10 days
and I spoke with one of the central banks

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:30.880
in one of the countries that has negative,
you know, interest rates.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:32.410
And I say, you have this limit.

00:09:32.410 --> 00:09:36.440
And I say, well, you could pass a law/regulation
that prevents the banks from switching from

00:09:36.440 --> 00:09:38.240
excess reserves into cash.

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:39.240
That's feasible, right?

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.220
You tell them you can have only as much cash
as you need for your own transaction.

00:09:41.220 --> 00:09:43.810
If you're a customer, tough luck, the rest
of it is held as like a sort of digital income

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:44.810
tax.

00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:46.870
It's much like-
BUITER: It doesn't mean you can't take it

00:09:46.870 --> 00:09:48.560
out and pass it on, you know?

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:52.680
You have-there is not just-you have to not
stop him from holding it, right?

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:57.360
You're actually stopping him from acting as
an intermediary between a non-bank guy who

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:58.680
is not affected by this and the central bank
itself.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:09:59.680
What the Swiss have is completely ineffective.

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:00.680
MALLABY: OK.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:01.680
OK, let's just switch it up for a second.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:02.680
LIPSKY: Nothing esoteric here.

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:03.680
(Laughter.)

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:04.680
MALLABY: I want a show of hands here.

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.530
Hands up who think it's plausible that in
the next five years we will either eliminate

00:10:09.530 --> 00:10:10.670
cash-
BUITER: Not in the U.S.

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:11.670
Not in the U.S.

00:10:11.670 --> 00:10:12.670
In Sweden.

00:10:12.670 --> 00:10:13.670
In Sweden they will do it.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:14.670
(Laughter.)

00:10:14.670 --> 00:10:15.670
Right?

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:16.670
MALLABY: Say a word about Sweden.

00:10:16.670 --> 00:10:20.519
BUITER: In Sweden, the ratio of cash to GDP-annual
GDP: 2, 2 1/2 percent.

00:10:20.519 --> 00:10:21.990
MALLABY: Last time I checked, Sweden was not
systemic.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:22.990
(Laughter.)

00:10:22.990 --> 00:10:23.990
Yeah, but-
BUITER: No, no, but they set the trend.

00:10:23.990 --> 00:10:24.990
They set the trend.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:28.370
ROUBINI: I mean, you know, the broader question-the
broader question, of course, is how-what you

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:32.470
do if you're going to hit regularly the zero
bound in the next recession or financial crisis.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.120
You have only a few options.

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:40.050
Either you go with the quantitative money-QE1-3
to infinity-or you go negative with the price

00:10:40.050 --> 00:10:41.519
of money, more negative interest rates.

00:10:41.519 --> 00:10:42.519
BUITER: Or helicopter money.

00:10:42.519 --> 00:10:45.830
ROUBINI: Or the environment of QE is just
helicopter and so on.

00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:49.230
Or third option is you change your inflation
target from 2 (percent) to 4 (percent) or

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:50.529
higher so you don't eat it.

00:10:50.529 --> 00:10:55.510
Or, if you believe the BS, you give up on
the 2 percent because the 0 percent is the

00:10:55.510 --> 00:10:58.060
new normal for the inflation target.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:01.980
You have to choose among-and each one of these
four options I would say has its own problems.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.990
But when I hit those zero bound over and over
again in the future, right?

00:11:05.990 --> 00:11:08.209
And that's the dilemma that all central banks
going to face for the next few years.

00:11:08.209 --> 00:11:09.209
MALLABY: John.

00:11:09.209 --> 00:11:10.209
John.

00:11:10.209 --> 00:11:11.230
LIPSKY: Let's see if I-let's see if I can
conclude.

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:16.540
So, despite all these considerations, right
now there seems to be a consensus that negative

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:18.780
rates aren't having that big an effect-
BUITER: Absolutely.

00:11:18.780 --> 00:11:21.040
LIPSKY: -nor are they likely to be much changed
in the near term.

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.740
BUITER: No, monetary policy in general.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:26.000
QE has very limited effects.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:29.950
Further rate cuts, either from positive to
less positive or from negative to more negative.

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:30.950
MALLABY: All right.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:31.950
BUITER: Look, monetary policy-monetary policy
is out of-

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:32.950
ROUBINI: Well, you know, no effect.

00:11:32.950 --> 00:11:36.800
I mean, I spoke with a central banker in a
country that's gone very negative, and they

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:39.879
said, well, what do you mean there's no effect?

00:11:39.879 --> 00:11:45.370
Our entire yield curve has flattened 20 (years),
30 (years), 40 years, 80 basis points down;

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:50.279
our 10-year bond yield is -10 basis points;
and it has a massive effect both on the steepening-

00:11:50.279 --> 00:11:51.279
BUITER: But that is not negative.

00:11:51.279 --> 00:11:52.279
QE does that.

00:11:52.279 --> 00:11:53.800
ROUBINI: -less steepening, and the yield curve
all the way sharply down.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:54.800
BUITER: No, no, no.

00:11:54.800 --> 00:11:57.079
ROUBINI: So the impact has been huge in terms
of financial regulations, right?

00:11:57.079 --> 00:11:58.079
BUITER: But that's-that's completely wrong.

00:11:58.079 --> 00:12:01.680
Negative rates, other things being equal,
steepen the yield curve.

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:03.900
It is QE that flattens the yield curve.

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:04.900
ROUBINI: No, no.

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:06.089
In Japan, the opposite occurred.

00:12:06.089 --> 00:12:09.360
When they went negative, the 40-year bond
yield fell 80 basis points and in 10 years

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:10.360
has gone to -10.

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:13.410
MALLABY: So what we're hearing is-
ROUBINI: So it's flattened and it's pushed

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:14.460
down the entire yield curve.

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:15.480
That's what happened in Japan.

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.490
MALLABY: -global monetary policy is obviously
in a bit of a fix-(laughter)-and in order

00:12:19.490 --> 00:12:24.959
to-and in order to get out of this fix, we
have to contemplate pretty amusing and radical

00:12:24.959 --> 00:12:25.959
things.

00:12:25.959 --> 00:12:27.840
So I want to throw out another radical one,
and I want you to answer this-okay?-with-I

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:33.050
don't want the sort of blanket like global
sort of it-doesn't-work story.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.970
Try and differentiate between the G-3.

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:41.680
So tell me, is this more plausible in Japan,
or Europe, or the U.S.? Which-because I think

00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:43.130
that's where it gets interesting.

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:48.420
Certain instruments-for example, simple outright
debt cancellation.

00:12:48.420 --> 00:12:54.330
So the Japanese central bank owns some insane
proportion of Japanese government bonds.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.380
They can cancel them.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.990
BUITER: Yeah, but that's-that makes no difference,
right?

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:00.640
That's just cosmetic stuff, right?

00:13:00.640 --> 00:13:04.290
The Japanese treasury-Ministry of Finance-is
already the beneficial owner of the Bank of

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:06.500
Japan, so they have to consolidate their balance
sheets.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.399
That is completely-that is just-
MALLABY: It's not complete-

00:13:08.399 --> 00:13:09.940
BUITER: It is pure tokenism, yeah.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:10.970
MALLABY: No, it's not.

00:13:10.970 --> 00:13:16.440
No, the implied space for fiscal stimulus-
BUITER: No implied space, because they assume

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:19.210
profits from the central bank or the treasury.

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:21.490
Only if you're myopic would that make any
difference.

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:22.610
MALLABY: OK, so that's negative.

00:13:22.610 --> 00:13:23.610
All right.

00:13:23.610 --> 00:13:24.980
So-(laughs)-
ROUBINI: Well, I would argue that the main

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:30.320
difference between QE and helicopter drop
is that one is supposed to be temporary-you

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:33.870
run up the balance sheet, you buy the bonds,
and you're supposed to sell them-and the other

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:35.640
one you have it permanent.

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:39.741
The tricky thing with doing it permanent is
if eventually-if eventually you achieve your

00:13:39.741 --> 00:13:44.360
inflation target and velocity rises, then
you have to mop up that excess liquidity.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:47.930
Otherwise, if credit growth is excessive,
we get inflation, and you can do it only by

00:13:47.930 --> 00:13:51.050
selling the bonds or by increasing the interest
on excess reserves.

00:13:51.050 --> 00:13:55.880
So the ability to having permanent QE, meaning
permanent copter money, assumes the demand

00:13:55.880 --> 00:13:59.260
for money is going to remain at this level
forever, and you'll never achieve the 2 percent

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:00.260
inflation.

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:01.990
BUITER: I, again, have to-
ROUBINI: Otherwise, you cannot have it permanent.

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:04.899
Eventually you have to go back to running
down the balance sheet or taxing excess reserve.

00:14:04.899 --> 00:14:07.810
BUITER: I again have to disagree with my colleague
here.

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.430
Helicopter money-it's not that it's permanent
or temporary.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:13.579
Helicopter money is additional monetary issues.

00:14:13.579 --> 00:14:18.329
It goes to fund fiscal stimulus that wouldn't-that
is, you can't affect a fiscal stimulus.

00:14:18.329 --> 00:14:19.870
It wouldn't have taken place otherwise.

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:21.980
ROUBINI: But then you're changing fiscal policy.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.840
I'm just saying, even with QE, you're financing
deficit.

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:25.840
BUITER: Yeah, but helicopter money is not
QE.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:26.840
ROUBINI: So you cannot change both.

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.030
You have to-you have to assume-
MALLABY: I want the audience to observe that-

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:33.440
ROUBINI: Of course, if you want to run larger
deficit, you could say we're going to then

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.440
print more money.

00:14:34.440 --> 00:14:35.440
BUITER: That's right.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:36.880
MALLABY: We have-hey, wait, wait-we have glasses
of water here and nobody-

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:37.880
ROUBINI: Conceptually.

00:14:37.880 --> 00:14:39.760
MALLABY: -nobody has thrown the water that
the other person yet.

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:40.760
(Laughter.)

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:43.810
So we're going to switch the subject a little
bit.

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:48.930
And I want to know whether the-and I'm going
to go to John first-

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:49.930
MR.

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:53.850
LIPSKY: Feeling I'm like John Kasich here.

00:14:53.850 --> 00:14:55.360
(Laughter.)

00:14:55.360 --> 00:15:05.041
MALLABY: So emerging markets were everybody's
big concern back in January or earlier, and

00:15:05.041 --> 00:15:08.269
now they've bounced back in terms of equity
performance.

00:15:08.269 --> 00:15:14.019
And I guess the question is, you know, is
that sustainable in the context of, you know,

00:15:14.019 --> 00:15:19.020
they did very well-the BRICS especially-in
a period of extraordinary bull market and

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.910
commodity prices, a sort of historic bull
market.

00:15:22.910 --> 00:15:25.240
Now that's gone, probably not coming back.

00:15:25.240 --> 00:15:27.010
Emerging-market productivity growth is down.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:30.810
What do you think the outlook is for the big
emerging markets?

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:31.810
MR.

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:35.700
LIPSKY: Well, I suspect one lesson we've learned
is that BRICS was nice acronym-(laughter)-but

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:41.029
it didn't explain much about what was going
on in the individual economies.

00:15:41.029 --> 00:15:46.570
Each one of those faces a very different set
of circumstances with different specific policy

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:47.590
challenges.

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:53.840
It's been clear that when the tide ran out
that some had lots more trouble-faced a lot

00:15:53.840 --> 00:15:55.210
more trouble than others.

00:15:55.210 --> 00:16:02.570
I think in general the case has been that
when times were good, the efforts at reform

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:04.070
were lacking.

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:09.490
Not shocking that politicians don't do hard
things when they don't have to.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:15.680
And now we're going to see-first of all, they're-why
things have bounced back a little bit is because

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:21.730
the sense is the world economy is not headed
into something disastrous, so commodity prices

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:27.699
are not going to do something terribly disastrous,
and we see oil coming back.

00:16:27.699 --> 00:16:35.290
But the-again, what we-what we do see is a
very significant downshift in medium- to long-term

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:40.949
expectations about growth prospects in these
countries absent some structural reforms.

00:16:40.949 --> 00:16:42.600
And so the test is on.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:45.500
The test is different in each one of those
countries.

00:16:45.500 --> 00:16:48.540
And there's reason to wonder how successful
they will be.

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.540
MALLABY: And, John, if you had to pick one
emerging market that does appear to be grappling

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:54.440
with structural reform, can you think of a
good example?

00:16:54.440 --> 00:17:01.040
LIPSKY: One of my colleagues may want to-I
think I would say right now they're all facing

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:02.769
substantial challenges.

00:17:02.769 --> 00:17:09.819
The ones-the economy that has a coherent and
thorough plan, in fact, is China.

00:17:09.819 --> 00:17:12.449
The question is, are they going to do it?

00:17:12.449 --> 00:17:18.000
They had the 12th five-year plan-was also
comprehensive and complete-and not all of

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:19.000
it was done.

00:17:19.000 --> 00:17:22.039
So now let's see if the 13th is done.

00:17:22.039 --> 00:17:30.200
Mr. Modi in India came in with a very ambitious
reform plan, and it's turned out to be harder

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:31.200
to do than anticipated.

00:17:31.200 --> 00:17:34.350
Now let's see if he really carries it-carries
it forward.

00:17:34.350 --> 00:17:39.251
But one of the large emergings-not one of
the BRICS-that has actually, I think, done

00:17:39.251 --> 00:17:45.380
more than people seem to recognize, and have
big problems but big prospect, is Indonesia.

00:17:45.380 --> 00:17:46.380
BUITER: I agree with that.

00:17:46.380 --> 00:17:48.370
I was going to mention it as well.

00:17:48.370 --> 00:17:50.240
Yeah, Modi, we have to wait, right?

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:57.560
Clearly, you know, came out of a state government
where he controlled both houses, and what

00:17:57.560 --> 00:17:58.570
he wanted happened.

00:17:58.570 --> 00:18:03.070
And he found out that if you don't control
the upper house, right, and if you can't abolish

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:08.160
it the way Mr. Renzi tries to do his, then,
no, you are likely-you have to compromise

00:18:08.160 --> 00:18:10.049
and negotiate, and it's been a complete debacle.

00:18:10.049 --> 00:18:13.830
So not even the sales tax-the regional sales
tax subsidy-

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:14.830
LIPSKY: GST.

00:18:14.830 --> 00:18:17.090
BUITER: -the GST-has been implemented.

00:18:17.090 --> 00:18:23.679
A bit of, you know-you're going to need massive
additional infrastructure investment; deregulation,

00:18:23.679 --> 00:18:28.860
especially of labor markets; increased FDI;
increased labor force participation by women;

00:18:28.860 --> 00:18:33.000
and increased educational standards for women,
especially in the countryside and all-et cetera,

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:34.270
et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:34.270 --> 00:18:39.820
I think the potential is enormous, and I have
greater hope for India than for China because

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:42.260
of China's governance system, right?

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:49.010
You can't imagine this becoming a happy, decentralized
market economy, as there is more centralization

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:53.870
of power going on now in decision-making competency
at any time since Mao, right?

00:18:53.870 --> 00:18:55.440
So I think it doesn't add up.

00:18:55.440 --> 00:19:00.750
And I'm very worried about the fact, also,
that wherever the power is getting more concentrated,

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:03.620
it doesn't seem to be really focused on the
economy.

00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:10.100
And that, I think, is a recipe for at least
a cyclical hard landing, which I think is

00:19:10.100 --> 00:19:15.650
still on the cards despite the slight recovery
we had in Q1, or rather a bottoming out of

00:19:15.650 --> 00:19:22.720
growth, which I think is the entirely predictable
response of the economy to old-style, excessive

00:19:22.720 --> 00:19:31.900
credit-growth driven, and heavy-lifting infrastructure-propelled
fiscal credit stimulus.

00:19:31.900 --> 00:19:37.260
Not sustainable, because it builds on the
unsustainable debt that China's already got,

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:40.870
but, you know, good in the short run.

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:46.340
But I think within two quarters we'll back
on this downward track.

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:50.611
MALLABY: So, Nouriel, the thing which interests
me a lot about the state of emerging markets

00:19:50.611 --> 00:19:58.630
right now is that you're running this experiment
where after the Asian crisis in the late '90s

00:19:58.630 --> 00:20:05.460
there were three lessons preached at emerging
markets: build up reserves, have a flexible

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:10.299
exchange rate, and don't have too much foreign-currency-denominated
debt.

00:20:10.299 --> 00:20:14.350
So I guess they ignored the last one, if you
look at corporate dollar debt in the emerging

00:20:14.350 --> 00:20:15.350
world.

00:20:15.350 --> 00:20:17.400
But they did the first two, by and large.

00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.650
More reserves, flexible currency.

00:20:19.650 --> 00:20:24.340
Do you think-obviously when you go through
a crunch like Brazil, for example, has gone

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:26.880
through recently, it feels horrific.

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:30.490
But do you think when we look back at this
in a couple of years' time, we'll feel as

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:31.530
though this experiment worked?

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:36.909
That the lessons were absorbed, applied, and
then emerging markets came through way better

00:20:36.909 --> 00:20:39.049
than they would have done in previous cycles.

00:20:39.049 --> 00:20:43.100
ROUBINI: Yeah, I mean, with the caveat that
you made at the beginning that third leg of

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:48.950
the great global deleveraging was in emerging
markets, that it went on a borrowing binge

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:52.470
when money was cheap-QE, zero rates-and was
going to emerging market, and commodity prices

00:20:52.470 --> 00:20:56.900
were high, and China was growing 10 percent.

00:20:56.900 --> 00:20:58.419
And now there is going to be a painful deleveraging.

00:20:58.419 --> 00:21:01.930
But I would say, compared to the past, with
exception, of course.

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:07.030
There are some cases in which a credit event
could occur-say, Venezuela, Ukraine, and so

00:21:07.030 --> 00:21:08.030
on.

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:13.110
But we've not had the kind of sudden stop
of capital, financial crisis, banking crisis,

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:17.980
sovereign-debt crisis that you saw in previous
generation of emerging market crises.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:23.000
And I would say, after those crises, of course,
there were some economic reforms, structural

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.429
and also macro, and better banks.

00:21:26.429 --> 00:21:30.659
But having exchange-rate flexibility and having
the buffer of reserves also has helped, and

00:21:30.659 --> 00:21:35.270
therefore we have not seen the kind of crisis,
with some exception, we've seen in the past,

00:21:35.270 --> 00:21:37.950
in spite of a buildup of a significant amount
of debt.

00:21:37.950 --> 00:21:42.220
And, by the way, not all of that debt, of
course, is in foreign currency.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:44.450
A lot of now is in local currency.

00:21:44.450 --> 00:21:47.100
And some of it is longer term rather than
shorter term.

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:51.799
So the kind of liquidity runs and currency
liquidity mismatches are not as severe as

00:21:51.799 --> 00:21:53.220
they were in the-in the '90s, for example.

00:21:53.220 --> 00:21:58.570
BUITER: Yeah, I think the lesson-don't defend
the indefensible, right, the fixed exchange

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:00.690
rates-has really been learned well.

00:22:00.690 --> 00:22:03.900
I think the greatest example, I think, is
Russia.

00:22:03.900 --> 00:22:09.270
OK, it's a supply-side economic disaster,
but they did everything right on the monetary,

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:10.940
fiscal, and exchange-rate front.

00:22:10.940 --> 00:22:16.010
Because Russia was not just hit by a single
whammy-the oil price going from, you know,

00:22:16.010 --> 00:22:21.060
120 (dollars) to whatever it was, 27 1/2 (dollars)
in a year and a half-but they got hit with

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:24.360
a double whammy, with the sanctions as well.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:27.530
So they should have done -10 percent, right?

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:28.559
Instead, what did they do?

00:22:28.559 --> 00:22:32.710
They took it on the exchange rate, but they
made sure that they were not going to have

00:22:32.710 --> 00:22:37.460
a complete collapse of the exchange rate,
which would have started a possible, you know,

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:41.030
inflation spiral, undermined political support
for the government and all that.

00:22:41.030 --> 00:22:45.340
So they raised rates to, you know, put a floor
under the exchange rate.

00:22:45.340 --> 00:22:48.580
And then, when the exchange rate stabilized,
they lowered rates again.

00:22:48.580 --> 00:22:50.010
Fiscal policy was roughly neutral.

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:56.179
I think it was really-they should give lessons
to the eurozone guys and the Japanese on how

00:22:56.179 --> 00:22:57.179
to manage macroeconomically.

00:22:57.179 --> 00:22:58.179
This was just right.

00:22:58.179 --> 00:23:01.610
ROUBINI: I mean, even a case-even a case that
was more messy, like Brazil, where there was

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:07.241
not the fiscal adjustment and everything is
political and otherwise messy, the risk of

00:23:07.241 --> 00:23:09.190
really-of a sudden stop of capital is limited.

00:23:09.190 --> 00:23:10.190
They have their reserves.

00:23:10.190 --> 00:23:12.890
They use the exchange rate as an absorbing
mechanism.

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:15.510
And therefore, even in this situation that's
very difficult, I don't think they'll have

00:23:15.510 --> 00:23:21.320
a run on the banks or run on the government,
or anything like a-(inaudible)-crisis.

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:22.320
It's highly unlikely.

00:23:22.320 --> 00:23:23.320
LIPSKY: All this is true.

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:25.910
And all this is good.

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:29.549
But you wouldn't want to miss another big
point, right, which is with regard to structural

00:23:29.549 --> 00:23:34.490
reform in these economies, what they've shown
is that with good short-term macro policies

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:36.480
they can run, but they can't hide.

00:23:36.480 --> 00:23:43.570
In the end if they can't produce total factor
productivity gains, then they're not going

00:23:43.570 --> 00:23:44.890
to get where they would like to go.

00:23:44.890 --> 00:23:51.360
BUITER: So Brazil, with the current supply
side, right, they have a potential upper growth

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:52.970
rate of, tops, 2 percent, right?

00:23:52.970 --> 00:23:55.770
These guys used to do 4 (percent) and 5 (percent),
right?

00:23:55.770 --> 00:24:00.650
Now, they need to open up the economy, which
is, I think the most closed economy-again,

00:24:00.650 --> 00:24:06.950
after North Korea-way more closed to trade
because of outright protectionism than any-in

00:24:06.950 --> 00:24:10.370
fact, than almost in any other South American
economy.

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:18.059
And, yeah, it's-they need, for the labor market,
the credit market-there's a dual credit market

00:24:18.059 --> 00:24:19.140
system, Chinese style.

00:24:19.140 --> 00:24:25.010
The lucky few-or, not a few-the lucky ones
borrow from the state-owned bank and from

00:24:25.010 --> 00:24:28.610
these other banks-(inaudible)-and the rest
borrows at twice what the-you know, the fair

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:34.690
rate would be as a result of that, and puts
themselves in a-in a horrendous debt situation.

00:24:34.690 --> 00:24:37.150
So it's a deeply distorted economy.

00:24:37.150 --> 00:24:40.120
But there's good news here.

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:45.620
It looks as though, you know, one of-at least
the government that's responsible for many

00:24:45.620 --> 00:24:48.500
of these distortions is on the way out.

00:24:48.500 --> 00:24:54.460
And so Brazil may have-if we have a general
election, right, which is not only possible

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:57.570
before the end of the year, then we have a
government that can focus on economic issues,

00:24:57.570 --> 00:24:59.130
rather than on survival.

00:24:59.130 --> 00:25:03.620
MALLABY: What's interesting here is that the
Washington consensus, sort of, you know, orthodox

00:25:03.620 --> 00:25:11.220
advice-which has been hugely maligned, particularly
since 2008-in this case was listened to, adopted-adopted

00:25:11.220 --> 00:25:16.790
notably by Russia, as you say, in a further
irony-and actually worked.

00:25:16.790 --> 00:25:21.260
And to the extent that there are problems
in emerging markets, it's the non-application

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:26.460
of further economic orthodoxy on the supply
side in terms of microeconomic reform.

00:25:26.460 --> 00:25:27.460
That's the problem.

00:25:27.460 --> 00:25:29.730
And the macro has been a success.

00:25:29.730 --> 00:25:34.049
ROUBINI: Yeah, but I would add, you know,
for the last decade, until '13, emerging markets

00:25:34.049 --> 00:25:35.049
were lucky.

00:25:35.049 --> 00:25:36.049
China was growing 10 percent.

00:25:36.049 --> 00:25:37.580
You had the commodity super cycle.

00:25:37.580 --> 00:25:40.360
You had zero rates and QEs all over the world.

00:25:40.360 --> 00:25:43.720
So there were-three major global tailwinds
are now headwinds.

00:25:43.720 --> 00:25:49.600
Too, in the easy years, a lot of this counted
on smarter fiscal and credit policy, twin

00:25:49.600 --> 00:25:54.180
current account and fiscal deficits, rode
falling inflation going up with the fragile

00:25:54.180 --> 00:25:57.929
five and a few others, and then most of them
did not do the structural reform.

00:25:57.929 --> 00:26:03.590
If anything, their model became state capitalism
and state-owned enterprises, state-owned banks,

00:26:03.590 --> 00:26:07.220
protectionism, resource national, you name
it, you know?

00:26:07.220 --> 00:26:11.210
It was in China, it was in Russia, it was
India until Modi, it was in Brazil, it was

00:26:11.210 --> 00:26:12.309
all over emerging market.

00:26:12.309 --> 00:26:13.990
And now they are paying the consequences.

00:26:13.990 --> 00:26:17.240
Yes, financially they are more stable because
of flexible exchange rates and reserves.

00:26:17.240 --> 00:26:19.990
BUITER: But there's nothing wrong with what-
ROUBINI: But they were lucky; when the tide

00:26:19.990 --> 00:26:21.529
turned around, they had the buffer.

00:26:21.529 --> 00:26:25.570
BUITER: The Washington consensus was right,
except they were probably too dogmatic on

00:26:25.570 --> 00:26:29.120
capital controls, which under certain circumstances
can be useful.

00:26:29.120 --> 00:26:30.610
But for the rest, yes.

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:36.490
I mean, it's-the so-called discrediting is
just the confused writings of-

00:26:36.490 --> 00:26:43.220
LIPSKY: Yeah, but don't forget, the folks
who originated the phase currency wars were

00:26:43.220 --> 00:26:49.419
the Brazilians, at a time when their fiscal
policy was as wrongheaded as it might have

00:26:49.419 --> 00:26:50.419
been.

00:26:50.419 --> 00:26:51.419
BUITER: Yeah, but that was not the Washington
consensus.

00:26:51.419 --> 00:26:52.419
(Laughs.)

00:26:52.419 --> 00:26:53.419
LIPSKY: Fair enough.

00:26:53.419 --> 00:26:54.419
Fair enough.

00:26:54.419 --> 00:26:55.720
MALLABY: Nouriel, you wanted to say something,
actually, about currency wars.

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:57.570
What's the next stage in that story?

00:26:57.570 --> 00:26:58.720
Because-
ROUBINI: Yeah.

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:04.720
Well, there were rumors that there was an
agreement at the G-20 in Shanghai that a stronger

00:27:04.720 --> 00:27:09.289
dollar was actually bad, was exacerbating
risk off, because stronger dollar implied

00:27:09.289 --> 00:27:11.900
a higher chance that China would do a step
deval.

00:27:11.900 --> 00:27:16.270
It was implying lower oil and commodity prices
that were leading then to credit spreads that

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:20.610
go through the roof, global equities falling,
and inflation expectation collapsing.

00:27:20.610 --> 00:27:24.820
And of course, strong dollar was weakening
U.S. equities and global.

00:27:24.820 --> 00:27:29.350
And I think that there was not a formal accord,
but there was an agreement that a stronger

00:27:29.350 --> 00:27:31.929
dollar would exacerbate this risk off.

00:27:31.929 --> 00:27:36.159
And risk off because risk on, in part because
the data out of China were not a disaster,

00:27:36.159 --> 00:27:40.600
in part because the Fed went very easy, in
part because U.S. was not faltering, but also

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:45.020
in part because the weakness of the dollar
could reverse all the cycle-stronger commodity

00:27:45.020 --> 00:27:50.530
prices, lower risk of China devaluing, and
global equities going up.

00:27:50.530 --> 00:27:56.150
But, by talking to European and Japanese officials
for the last few days, I got the sense that

00:27:56.150 --> 00:28:01.549
this accord has become a discord, because
European and Japanese say, wait, after Shanghai

00:28:01.549 --> 00:28:05.559
we play by the rules of the game, U.S. growth
went higher, U.S. inflation and inflation

00:28:05.559 --> 00:28:10.070
expectation went higher, while in Europe and
Japan growth has gone lower and inflation

00:28:10.070 --> 00:28:11.649
expectation are going lower.

00:28:11.649 --> 00:28:14.670
In the meanwhile, the U.S. stock market has
gone back to what it was before, while in

00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:15.940
Europe and Japan it is even lower.

00:28:15.940 --> 00:28:20.030
And in spite of all these things, the dollar
now is weakening, and that's really killing

00:28:20.030 --> 00:28:22.820
us in terms of achieving our growth and inflation
target.

00:28:22.820 --> 00:28:25.100
And what happened was that the Fed went very
dovish.

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:26.580
The FOMC, then Janet Yellen.

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:31.070
There are some Fed officials actually actively
in their speeches talking down the dollar.

00:28:31.070 --> 00:28:36.420
So a senior ECB official told me in private,
first of all, Janet Yellen should reign in

00:28:36.420 --> 00:28:39.200
her troops, meaning telling them to shut up
and not talking down the dollar.

00:28:39.200 --> 00:28:43.470
And there was a threat: for the first time
I heard the ECB official saying, if that doesn't

00:28:43.470 --> 00:28:48.110
happen and there's not an agreement this weekend
in Washington, we could do QE with foreign

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:49.110
assets.

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:51.919
It was a polite word for forex intervention
unilaterally.

00:28:51.919 --> 00:28:55.669
And if the ECB thinks this way, BOJ, where
the yen has gone up even stronger and where

00:28:55.669 --> 00:28:58.890
growth and inflation are even worse, is even
more upset about it.

00:28:58.890 --> 00:29:00.800
So I don't think it's going to happen.

00:29:00.800 --> 00:29:04.990
But I'd say that if the dollar were to keep
on weakening and euro and yen keep appreciating,

00:29:04.990 --> 00:29:09.409
apart from unconventional policy-BOJ doing
more negative policy rates in April-they may

00:29:09.409 --> 00:29:11.620
not have the time to wait until the end of
April.

00:29:11.620 --> 00:29:14.450
They might have to act earlier, because this
is becoming a serious issue.

00:29:14.450 --> 00:29:17.700
So the agreement has become a massive disagreement.

00:29:17.700 --> 00:29:22.340
Now, if you ask the Americans about it, they
tell you, wait a moment, the strong dollar

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:27.270
was causing risk off, so Europeans and Japanese
should be happy they're now in risk on.

00:29:27.270 --> 00:29:33.200
Two, we are in a situation in which, if growth
is weak in the eurozone, you can use fiscal

00:29:33.200 --> 00:29:37.679
policy rather than using exchange-rate and
beggar-thy-neighbor policies.

00:29:37.679 --> 00:29:41.700
And, three, Germany's running 8 percent of
GDP current account surplus.

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:45.390
They can raise the wages, they can cut taxes,
they can do lots of things.

00:29:45.390 --> 00:29:48.039
And the eurozone overall is having also a
surplus.

00:29:48.039 --> 00:29:50.600
So I think there's a conflict on currency
right now.

00:29:50.600 --> 00:29:53.730
There's a conflict on monetary policy, also
a conflict on fiscal policy.

00:29:53.730 --> 00:29:58.320
Within the G-7, some people hope that there'll
be an agreement on more loose fiscal policy

00:29:58.320 --> 00:29:59.660
in exchange for less monetary.

00:29:59.660 --> 00:30:02.860
Germany is against it and the U.K. is against
it.

00:30:02.860 --> 00:30:06.350
So, like at the G-20, we're not going to get
an agreement that resolves this tension by

00:30:06.350 --> 00:30:07.679
doing more fiscal.

00:30:07.679 --> 00:30:11.480
So I think this is a sense in which things
are difficult right now.

00:30:11.480 --> 00:30:16.240
BUITER: There never was a Shanghai accord,
not even an implicit understanding.

00:30:16.240 --> 00:30:22.080
Each nation has since then, and before then,
done exactly what it wanted for the, you know,

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:26.289
perceived national interest, no attempt at
coordination or assisting.

00:30:26.289 --> 00:30:28.990
It hasn't always worked out the way they hoped.

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:32.630
The Japanese certainly didn't expect that
after cutting rates, right, the day after

00:30:32.630 --> 00:30:34.419
their currency would appreciate.

00:30:34.419 --> 00:30:39.420
And this reversal of the normal laws of economics,
which actually is not quite as crazy if you

00:30:39.420 --> 00:30:43.950
look at what else changed at the time, but
there is not a sense that the people are pursuing

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:44.950
this.

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:50.390
The Fed did not talk more dovishly, right,
than they did-in February and since-than they

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:52.730
did in December, because-
ROUBINI: They did.

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.850
The Fed has become extremely dovish by any
standard.

00:30:55.850 --> 00:30:58.350
They went from-(inaudible)-of four hikes to
two.

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:00.029
The FOMC was dovish.

00:31:00.029 --> 00:31:02.270
Janet Yellen in her speech was even more dovish.

00:31:02.270 --> 00:31:04.320
And some Fed officials are even more so.

00:31:04.320 --> 00:31:05.320
BUITER: But that has-that has nothing to do
with-that has nothing to with international-I

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:06.320
agree.

00:31:06.320 --> 00:31:08.190
They are very dovish, but it is for domestic
reasons, because-

00:31:08.190 --> 00:31:09.549
ROUBINI: It's not just domestic.

00:31:09.549 --> 00:31:11.820
Janet spoke about international factors.

00:31:11.820 --> 00:31:14.640
The view was a stronger dollar was causing
risk off.

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:15.940
We shouldn't have a stronger dollar.

00:31:15.940 --> 00:31:16.940
MALLABY: This is another real disagreement.

00:31:16.940 --> 00:31:17.940
We can just-
BUITER: I think this is not-

00:31:17.940 --> 00:31:19.419
ROUBINI: There was an agreement-
BUITER: There was no agreement.

00:31:19.419 --> 00:31:22.420
ROUBINI: -that was tacit on preventing further
dollar appreciation.

00:31:22.420 --> 00:31:26.270
The problem has gone all the way the other
direction and European and Japanese are pissed

00:31:26.270 --> 00:31:27.270
off about it.

00:31:27.270 --> 00:31:28.610
MALLABY: But whether there was an agreement
or not-

00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:29.610
ROUBINI: And they are saying so.

00:31:29.610 --> 00:31:36.029
MALLABY: -what's interesting is that now there
is-if it's true that there is a discord-(laughter,

00:31:36.029 --> 00:31:37.100
applause)-
ROUBINI: There is a discord.

00:31:37.100 --> 00:31:42.110
BUITER: And there will be further-there will
be further cuts-rate cuts in the euro area.

00:31:42.110 --> 00:31:44.110
There will be further rate cuts in Japan.

00:31:44.110 --> 00:31:48.630
And the Fed will sit on its hands as long
as the American economy shows sign of the

00:31:48.630 --> 00:31:50.669
kind of weakness we had in this first quarter.

00:31:50.669 --> 00:31:51.669
MALLABY: John.

00:31:51.669 --> 00:31:55.810
LIPSKY: Well, first of all, Sebastian, I guess
you're getting ready to moderate a Republican

00:31:55.810 --> 00:31:56.810
debate.

00:31:56.810 --> 00:31:57.810
(Laughter.)

00:31:57.810 --> 00:32:02.700
ROUBINI: Well, Willem and I are good friends
and we were colleagues at Yale.

00:32:02.700 --> 00:32:03.700
LIPSKY: We're all good friends.

00:32:03.700 --> 00:32:04.860
ROUBINI: I'd say Willem hired me, so-(laughter)-ever-grateful.

00:32:04.860 --> 00:32:07.269
MALLABY: All right, so now that we have the
backstory.

00:32:07.269 --> 00:32:09.880
Let's talk about some-
LIPSKY: All right, but I just want to say-

00:32:09.880 --> 00:32:13.289
MALLABY: Academic disputes are so vicious
because the stakes are so low.

00:32:13.289 --> 00:32:14.289
ROUBINI: Exactly.

00:32:14.289 --> 00:32:15.289
LIPSKY: Right.

00:32:15.289 --> 00:32:16.289
(Laughter.)

00:32:16.289 --> 00:32:21.440
The idea that these-that international policy
agreements or cooperation is like a football

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:27.840
huddle-American football huddle-in which you're
going to go down and cut left, and you're

00:32:27.840 --> 00:32:29.179
going to fake right.

00:32:29.179 --> 00:32:31.519
And it's not going to happen.

00:32:31.519 --> 00:32:37.330
It's never going to happen, except in the
most exceptional and emergency situations,

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:39.179
like in 2008.

00:32:39.179 --> 00:32:45.570
Instead, the idea, which was created in-remember,
the principle institutional response to the

00:32:45.570 --> 00:32:49.970
global financial crisis was the creation of
the G-20 at the leaders level.

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:57.720
And in September 2009 at Pittsburgh, at the-promoted
mainly by the Americans, the creation of the

00:32:57.720 --> 00:33:02.029
so-called Framework for Strong, Sustaining
and Balanced Growth, which created a framework

00:33:02.029 --> 00:33:11.149
working group that has produced detailed plans
for policy consistency and cooperation that

00:33:11.149 --> 00:33:17.080
the idea is that exactly we should be following
macro policy that seem mutually consistent

00:33:17.080 --> 00:33:19.190
and supportive of global stability.

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:21.440
At the technical level, this is happening.

00:33:21.440 --> 00:33:27.680
And in fact, in Melbourne at the last G-20,
as in every G-20 leader's summit, there's

00:33:27.680 --> 00:33:31.730
the adoption of an action plan of things that
are to be done.

00:33:31.730 --> 00:33:36.460
My guess is none of you have read the Melbourne
action plan, may not have ever heard of the

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:38.049
Melbourne action plan.

00:33:38.049 --> 00:33:42.210
There's going to be a Chinese action plan-
BUITER: My God.

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:44.630
LIPSKY: Yes, but agreed by the G-20.

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:51.330
The problem is, at the political level, you
haven't heard any commitment to a process

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:55.919
of making macro policies and structural policies
mutually consistent.

00:33:55.919 --> 00:34:02.370
And that has robbed confidence and led to
these kinds of unrealistic expectation that

00:34:02.370 --> 00:34:07.050
somehow that cooperation's going to involve
next Thursday you do this, and then Friday

00:34:07.050 --> 00:34:09.662
I'm going to do that, and then next Monday
I'm going to do the other.

00:34:09.662 --> 00:34:14.859
BUITER: I have only seen one example since
the great financial crisis started of international

00:34:14.859 --> 00:34:15.859
coordination.

00:34:15.859 --> 00:34:23.399
One act of international-of one side-unselfishness,
intelligent unselfishness, that is the Fed

00:34:23.399 --> 00:34:27.190
making its help lines available, was I think
the most important act in keeping Europe alive.

00:34:27.190 --> 00:34:32.179
The only act of cooperation or coordination
was the simultaneous rate cut after Lehman,

00:34:32.179 --> 00:34:33.179
right?

00:34:33.179 --> 00:34:39.020
And then we had-there was supposed to be fiscal
coordination in the November 2008 meeting.

00:34:39.020 --> 00:34:45.340
What there was was the coordinated announcement
of national fiscal policies, all of them expansionary,

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:50.060
right, which had already been agreed on without
any consideration for their internal coherence.

00:34:50.060 --> 00:34:54.330
So it was-there was no coordination of policies;
they just happened to all be in London at

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.339
the time and said put it on a sheet.

00:34:56.339 --> 00:34:57.840
So there has been no cooperation.

00:34:57.840 --> 00:35:01.050
MALLABY: OK, so, reading from my notes here,
which are always formally prepared by the

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:06.330
Council on Foreign Relations, it says: At
this I would like to invite members to join-to

00:35:06.330 --> 00:35:07.570
join the mosh pit-(laughter)-
BUITER: In prayer.

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:08.570
(Laughter.)

00:35:08.570 --> 00:35:09.570
MALLABY: -with their questions.

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:12.750
So if anyone has a question.

00:35:12.750 --> 00:35:18.410
Yes, I see one right over there.

00:35:18.410 --> 00:35:19.410
Earl.

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:20.410
Remember, this is on the record.

00:35:20.410 --> 00:35:21.410
Q: Thank you.

00:35:21.410 --> 00:35:22.410
Earl Carr, representing Momentum Advisors,
based in New York City.

00:35:22.410 --> 00:35:23.410
And thank you for a very lively discussion.

00:35:23.410 --> 00:35:24.560
BUITER: We do our best.

00:35:24.560 --> 00:35:25.560
(Laughter.)

00:35:25.560 --> 00:35:27.750
Q: As you know, it's an election year in the
United States.

00:35:27.750 --> 00:35:28.750
BUITER: Really?

00:35:28.750 --> 00:35:29.750
(Laughter.)

00:35:29.750 --> 00:35:35.420
Q: And the four leading candidates are all
opposed to the Trans-Pacific Trade Partnership.

00:35:35.420 --> 00:35:41.560
If TPP is not passed, how important does that
affect global growth?

00:35:41.560 --> 00:35:44.080
If it is passed, how important is it as well?

00:35:44.080 --> 00:35:45.930
MALLABY: Who wants to take that?

00:35:45.930 --> 00:35:47.780
TPP, how much does it matter?

00:35:47.780 --> 00:35:51.260
ROUBINI: Well, you know, whether it's passed
or not depends, of course, on who is going

00:35:51.260 --> 00:35:52.260
to win the election.

00:35:52.260 --> 00:35:56.609
I mean, Hillary Clinton has said there are
certain things she doesn't like about it,

00:35:56.609 --> 00:36:02.890
but I think that if she were to win, probably
could be passed in the lame-duck session between

00:36:02.890 --> 00:36:05.079
the election and the new presidency.

00:36:05.079 --> 00:36:07.480
That's the window at which you could have
a chance of doing it.

00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:10.050
So I think there is still a hope it's going
to be passed.

00:36:10.050 --> 00:36:16.540
You know, usually people say that the trade
agreements don't have a huge impact.

00:36:16.540 --> 00:36:20.450
If you do the general equilibrium on GDP it's
a positive, but it's modest.

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:25.340
And also, by the way, winners and losers-you
have to make sure that you compensate the

00:36:25.340 --> 00:36:26.890
losers, because otherwise you have a backlash.

00:36:26.890 --> 00:36:32.540
And I see the Brexit risk in part driven by
those who are losers from trade and globalization

00:36:32.540 --> 00:36:34.420
saying enough of Europe and blaming it on
Europe.

00:36:34.420 --> 00:36:38.110
So you have to think about those questions.

00:36:38.110 --> 00:36:43.329
But I would say that TPP has also a geopolitical
dimension.

00:36:43.329 --> 00:36:49.730
You know, without TPP, the pivot of the United
States towards Asia is empty.

00:36:49.730 --> 00:36:51.720
There is a rising China.

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:56.349
And the U.S. wants, if not to contain it,
to make sure that it doesn't become too aggressive.

00:36:56.349 --> 00:37:02.110
I would say that the right solution eventually
will be TPP is passed, China join TPP, and

00:37:02.110 --> 00:37:07.170
the U.S. joints the AIIB, and we have a greater
amount of both trade, investment, and so on

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:08.170
around the world.

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:09.170
And I hope that that's going to happen.

00:37:09.170 --> 00:37:12.930
Whether it's going to happen or not is going
to take time.

00:37:12.930 --> 00:37:20.960
LIPSKY: Well, I would like to say the following:
First of all, TPP is at best a second best.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:25.790
And TTIP, actually, I think is probably more
important from the U.S. point of view, even

00:37:25.790 --> 00:37:29.760
though it's taken-most people don't even know
about it or think about it.

00:37:29.760 --> 00:37:34.880
But the big picture here, is we seem to have
abandoned multilateral trade negotiations.

00:37:34.880 --> 00:37:36.859
We abandoned Doha.

00:37:36.859 --> 00:37:42.020
And I suspect it is not-it's not the only
cause, but as you've all heard in the last

00:37:42.020 --> 00:37:49.020
few years since the crisis, global trade has
grown at a pace slower than the global economy.

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:53.260
And I think the fact that there has not been
a multilateral trade agreement for the last

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:58.950
20 years or so is not irrelevant for this
gradual slowdown in the growth of world trade,

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:03.470
that was associated with better global-and
better balanced global growth.

00:38:03.470 --> 00:38:10.670
So I find the current political debate on
trade policy at best disappointing, and at

00:38:10.670 --> 00:38:14.590
worst disconcerting, and a little bit terrifying.

00:38:14.590 --> 00:38:21.030
Because if we're going to-if the United States
is going to decide that we're against new

00:38:21.030 --> 00:38:25.480
progress on trade liberalization, then I would
think that we've got many more risks.

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:28.700
MALLABY: But I want to bring Willem in on
this one, because he's a reliable contrarian.

00:38:28.700 --> 00:38:32.230
But so here is what I'm going to invite you
to comment on.

00:38:32.230 --> 00:38:38.280
So Nouriel rightly points out that, you know,
the effects on growth of TPP are positive

00:38:38.280 --> 00:38:44.680
but small, in the models, and that there's
a distributional consequence and you should

00:38:44.680 --> 00:38:45.900
deal with that.

00:38:45.900 --> 00:38:51.330
But we've run that experiment, and the political
system is very bad at dealing with the distributional

00:38:51.330 --> 00:38:52.330
consequences.

00:38:52.330 --> 00:38:56.030
So you then get negative political results
from these trade agreements.

00:38:56.030 --> 00:39:02.990
Isn't there an argument for saying, look,
for this rather modest economic gain, the

00:39:02.990 --> 00:39:04.869
political costs are too high.

00:39:04.869 --> 00:39:10.710
And you know, this globalization experiment
has been pushed a long way, further than the

00:39:10.710 --> 00:39:14.780
politics is willing to tolerate, and it's
time to cool it?

00:39:14.780 --> 00:39:16.610
BUITER: Not at all.

00:39:16.610 --> 00:39:20.800
I would like to see a TWP, a Trans-World Partnership,
rather than a TPP.

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:26.350
But this is the best we can have in this,
I think, increasingly de-globalizing world.

00:39:26.350 --> 00:39:29.010
It's not just to the trade field.

00:39:29.010 --> 00:39:34.650
You know, the welcome extended to immigrants
everywhere is also getting increasingly worn,

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:37.599
you notice, both in Europe and America.

00:39:37.599 --> 00:39:43.550
So it really is-I think we are at risk of
giving up all the gains from globalization,

00:39:43.550 --> 00:39:45.150
which have been massive.

00:39:45.150 --> 00:39:48.280
And the reason is, of course, that-one of
the reasons it; there are other reasons-but

00:39:48.280 --> 00:39:53.751
that we have no internal mechanisms for compensating
the losers.

00:39:53.751 --> 00:39:57.020
And the U.S. is especially poorly placed there.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:02.880
At least in Europe there is a tradition of
an active redistributive state, right?

00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:06.440
In America, that is much more-much less developed.

00:40:06.440 --> 00:40:15.900
So I think that, in terms of the ability to
ultimately get rid of the populist, nativist,

00:40:15.900 --> 00:40:25.520
nationalist, regionalist, localist, you know,
eruptions in the West, I think that Europe

00:40:25.520 --> 00:40:31.320
probably has a better chance than America,
because there is an internal redistributive

00:40:31.320 --> 00:40:35.839
ethic that does not-you know, does not exist
in this country.

00:40:35.839 --> 00:40:42.480
So I'm quite worried about the ability of
the U.S. to do anything other than say, well,

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:43.570
shucks, tough luck.

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:45.350
You know, Detroit-
(Audio break.)

00:40:45.350 --> 00:40:50.780
MALLABY: So the paradox here is the more you
reduce boundaries between states, the more

00:40:50.780 --> 00:40:52.270
you have to empower states.

00:40:52.270 --> 00:40:56.589
LIPSKY: Well, but, Sebastian-
BUITER: Or allow people to move.

00:40:56.589 --> 00:40:58.970
LIPSKY: Just take your-take your discussion,
flip it around.

00:40:58.970 --> 00:41:01.290
What if-what if WTO had been rejected?

00:41:01.290 --> 00:41:03.829
Would we be in a better place?

00:41:03.829 --> 00:41:05.140
I don't think so.

00:41:05.140 --> 00:41:06.140
That happened in the '90s.

00:41:06.140 --> 00:41:11.630
We tend to look back on the 1990s as a time
of great prosperity in the U.S., and success,

00:41:11.630 --> 00:41:16.670
and improved productivity, right, and good
wage growth.

00:41:16.670 --> 00:41:22.730
That came in the wake of the last significant
agreement on trade liberalization.

00:41:22.730 --> 00:41:30.030
So what we're missing here is a lot of this
discussion reflects not the lack of compensatory

00:41:30.030 --> 00:41:37.220
measures, which are always difficult, but
the sluggish growth in the overall economy.

00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:42.510
In the past, relatively rapid growth compensated
politically for these kind of problems.

00:41:42.510 --> 00:41:43.510
MALLABY: OK.

00:41:43.510 --> 00:41:46.920
All right, let's go to the lady on the aisle
there.

00:41:46.920 --> 00:41:48.220
Thank you.

00:41:48.220 --> 00:41:52.770
Q: Lyric Hughes Hale from Chicago.

00:41:52.770 --> 00:41:54.600
This Sunday, OPEC is meeting.

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:56.410
Freeze, no freeze?

00:41:56.410 --> 00:42:00.310
Why haven't lower oil prices been more simulative?

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:01.990
What do you expect to happen going forward?

00:42:01.990 --> 00:42:04.520
MALLABY: Who wants to take that?

00:42:04.520 --> 00:42:09.849
BUITER: Well, first, I expect that the meeting
this weekend will be like all the meetings

00:42:09.849 --> 00:42:10.849
before it.

00:42:10.849 --> 00:42:18.400
Nothing will happen, because it is basically
not in anybody's interest to do-that's in

00:42:18.400 --> 00:42:21.850
a position to do impose a solution to do anything.

00:42:21.850 --> 00:42:27.810
Saudi is key here, and they have changed their
view on peak oil from peak supply to peak

00:42:27.810 --> 00:42:29.200
demand, right?

00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:34.920
And they are-they feel themselves at risk
of being stuck with a stranded asset, and

00:42:34.920 --> 00:42:39.660
they'd rather pump and sell it at 30 (dollars)
than keep it in the ground and sell it at

00:42:39.660 --> 00:42:40.660
zero 10 years from now.

00:42:40.660 --> 00:42:41.930
So I think it's not going to happen.

00:42:41.930 --> 00:42:50.630
Why there hasn't been more of a-of a lift,
well, first instance, right, look at consumption.

00:42:50.630 --> 00:42:54.900
And oil price decline, like an oil price increase,
is purely redistributive.

00:42:54.900 --> 00:43:00.420
So there should be neither-if the losers spent
the same as the winners, there should be no

00:43:00.420 --> 00:43:01.420
net effect.

00:43:01.420 --> 00:43:08.290
Now, I think clearly, even on the consumption
side, if the decline, in this case, is totally

00:43:08.290 --> 00:43:09.950
unexpected as was, right?

00:43:09.950 --> 00:43:17.700
If anybody told me in June 2014 in January
2016 you're going to sell oil at 37 bucks

00:43:17.700 --> 00:43:20.730
a barrel, I'd have said, come on, pull the
other one, right?

00:43:20.730 --> 00:43:22.690
So totally unexpected.

00:43:22.690 --> 00:43:26.080
Large and very disruptive, right?

00:43:26.080 --> 00:43:33.230
So clearly I would say, you know, the losers
have probably cut back their spending more

00:43:33.230 --> 00:43:34.230
than the winners.

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:35.650
But then there's the investment dimension.

00:43:35.650 --> 00:43:38.000
And there, of course, the asymmetry is even
greater.

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:46.160
In the U.S., you know, the oil sector, you
know, is the late and lamented oil sector.

00:43:46.160 --> 00:43:48.640
And so CAPEX is gone.

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:56.830
Now, 97 percent of U.S. GDP is produced by
sectors that use oil and gas as an input,

00:43:56.830 --> 00:43:58.880
so they should benefit from this.

00:43:58.880 --> 00:44:05.230
But I think they want to see that this price
decline is permanent, and that the recovery

00:44:05.230 --> 00:44:10.119
to 40-ish (dollars) that we're seeing is not
the beginning of a crawl back up the hill.

00:44:10.119 --> 00:44:11.119
I don't think it will be.

00:44:11.119 --> 00:44:23.339
I, myself, think that, between Iran, Libya,
and the interests of the Gulf States in keeping

00:44:23.339 --> 00:44:30.600
the spending up, and the total lack of interest,
I think, of Russia in any fundamental agreement,

00:44:30.600 --> 00:44:33.670
we are not going to see any serious recovery
in oil.

00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:39.869
And the benefits, I think, will become more
visible as time passes, and we get the very

00:44:39.869 --> 00:44:47.369
widely diffused investment benefits from the
lower oil price, as well as, I think, a normal

00:44:47.369 --> 00:44:48.540
consumption response.

00:44:48.540 --> 00:44:51.490
MALLABY: Let's go in the back there.

00:44:51.490 --> 00:44:52.490
Lady in the white.

00:44:52.490 --> 00:44:55.099
BUITER: But Mr. Roubini, I think, was going
to disagree with me.

00:44:55.099 --> 00:44:56.349
ROUBINI: No, other than-
Q: Hi.

00:44:56.349 --> 00:44:57.349
Claire Casey, Garten Rothkopf.

00:44:57.349 --> 00:44:58.349
MALLABY: All right, start again.

00:44:58.349 --> 00:44:59.349
BUITER: Not this again.

00:44:59.349 --> 00:45:00.349
(Laughs.)

00:45:00.349 --> 00:45:01.349
Q: Sorry.

00:45:01.349 --> 00:45:02.349
Claire Casey, Garten Rothkopf.

00:45:02.349 --> 00:45:04.150
There seems to be a growing body of literature
saying the reason governments are having such

00:45:04.150 --> 00:45:07.950
a hard time managing this economic situation
is because it's actually the models are broken,

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:14.040
and even the tools we use to measure productivity,
like GDP, fail to capture large elements of

00:45:14.040 --> 00:45:15.800
our economies, particularly the digital economy.

00:45:15.800 --> 00:45:19.560
Could you comment on that, and how relevant
it is to this discussion?

00:45:19.560 --> 00:45:24.119
MALLABY: So undermeasurement of productivity
in the tech sector.

00:45:24.119 --> 00:45:26.290
BUITER: A bit, but it's not a major issue.

00:45:26.290 --> 00:45:27.290
ROUBINI: Yeah.

00:45:27.290 --> 00:45:30.349
I mean, there's a bit of a paradox, because
when you go to, say, Silicon Valley, you speak

00:45:30.349 --> 00:45:36.090
to these innovators, they say, we have innovation
like we have never seen it before-you know,

00:45:36.090 --> 00:45:40.579
energy technologies, biotechnology, information
technologies, manufacturing technologies,

00:45:40.579 --> 00:45:48.760
robotic automation, AI, 3D printing, personalized
computing, whatever, even financial-fintech

00:45:48.760 --> 00:45:51.860
technologies, defense technologies-so they
say we're on the verge of innovation that

00:45:51.860 --> 00:45:53.900
are going to change the world for the better.

00:45:53.900 --> 00:45:55.520
In the macro data, you don't see it.

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:58.710
So I would say there are several interpretations.

00:45:58.710 --> 00:46:03.450
One is that Bob Gordon (usually ?) says all
these things are gimmicks; all these apps

00:46:03.450 --> 00:46:07.359
we use we make life a little bit easier, but
compared to the major revolution occur in

00:46:07.359 --> 00:46:10.190
the past, that they're not significant.

00:46:10.190 --> 00:46:15.400
Second explanation, I think, is there is a
lag, even during the first Internet revolution.

00:46:15.400 --> 00:46:19.310
Only when these apps went from B to B, to
B to C, and eventually from the tech sector

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:23.579
spread to the economy, you saw an increase
in productivity.

00:46:23.579 --> 00:46:27.050
The third explanation might be that we are
mismeasuring it.

00:46:27.050 --> 00:46:30.990
Probably there is some mismeasurement, but
you'll have to argue that the mismeasurement

00:46:30.990 --> 00:46:34.990
that was also in the past has gotten worse
than it was before to argue that this is a

00:46:34.990 --> 00:46:37.540
significant factor.

00:46:37.540 --> 00:46:40.430
So the honest answer is with a no.

00:46:40.430 --> 00:46:41.430
(Laughter.)

00:46:41.430 --> 00:46:47.500
BUITER: There is a bit-you can give up an
example of the fact that quality improvements

00:46:47.500 --> 00:46:51.240
in services are especially hard to measure.

00:46:51.240 --> 00:46:55.329
Hedonic price indices, which are used in principle,
right, are terrible.

00:46:55.329 --> 00:46:58.890
And then on top of that, of course, every
advanced country in the world has cut back

00:46:58.890 --> 00:47:00.910
on its spending on statistical services.

00:47:00.910 --> 00:47:06.201
So the quality of the data is not deliberately
doctored, don't get me wrong, but they're

00:47:06.201 --> 00:47:09.339
just poor quality because you get what you
pay for.

00:47:09.339 --> 00:47:17.960
Now, an example is, if you look at the price
indices for software-for packaged, standardized

00:47:17.960 --> 00:47:22.920
software-there they do quality adjustment,
hedonic indices.

00:47:22.920 --> 00:47:27.470
And they don't do it for made-to-measure software.

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:34.880
If you simply assume that the quality improvements,
right, in the made-to-measure software is

00:47:34.880 --> 00:47:40.470
the same as in the packaged software, then
you add about 0.2 (percent), 0.3 percent a

00:47:40.470 --> 00:47:41.580
year to GDP growth.

00:47:41.580 --> 00:47:44.450
So that's just-but it's not the end of the
world, right?

00:47:44.450 --> 00:47:45.900
But it's quite interesting.

00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:52.240
For the rest, I think that the Gordon notion
that the, you know, knowledge-the tree of

00:47:52.240 --> 00:47:55.210
knowledge metaphor of innovation: the low
hanging fruit has been plucked.

00:47:55.210 --> 00:47:56.630
We've done fire.

00:47:56.630 --> 00:47:57.849
We've done water, right?

00:47:57.849 --> 00:47:58.849
(Laughter.)

00:47:58.849 --> 00:47:59.890
We've done the horse.

00:47:59.890 --> 00:48:03.579
We've done, you know, motion, power.

00:48:03.579 --> 00:48:09.500
And then all we have left now is boring old,
you know, second-generation IT, artificial

00:48:09.500 --> 00:48:11.099
intelligence, robotics, and bioengineering.

00:48:11.099 --> 00:48:15.090
And no, that's not-I think it is complete
baloney.

00:48:15.090 --> 00:48:17.290
These things are so exciting.

00:48:17.290 --> 00:48:21.660
The problem is, in order to see it in the
data-in any data-you have to incorporate the

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:25.450
new innovations in people, products, process,
and capital.

00:48:25.450 --> 00:48:27.750
And we're not investing, right?

00:48:27.750 --> 00:48:28.960
Not in people or in capital.

00:48:28.960 --> 00:48:29.960
So of course they don't see it.

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:34.359
MALLABY: There's also-the business model is
often to give away new tech products like

00:48:34.359 --> 00:48:38.240
search functions or any number of apps you
can think of on your phone.

00:48:38.240 --> 00:48:40.960
And if you give it away, it's not in GDP.

00:48:40.960 --> 00:48:42.160
BUITER: But that's correct.

00:48:42.160 --> 00:48:45.130
ROUBINI: Yeah, the other comment I will make
is eventually there will be an increase in

00:48:45.130 --> 00:48:46.130
productivity.

00:48:46.130 --> 00:48:51.300
The question's going to be then, however,
that we know technology is increasingly capital-intensive,

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:53.070
skill bias, and labor saving.

00:48:53.070 --> 00:48:56.520
And we-already there has been an increase
in inequality in part driven by trade and

00:48:56.520 --> 00:48:57.520
globalization.

00:48:57.520 --> 00:49:01.720
I think technology might be actually-under
some circumstances it might be a driver of

00:49:01.720 --> 00:49:02.880
that rise in inequality.

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:05.119
LIPSKY: Quickly, but I must say-
ROUBINI: But that's going to feed into this

00:49:05.119 --> 00:49:07.410
backlash against trade-
BUITER: Remember, in a world where-

00:49:07.410 --> 00:49:11.410
ROUBINI: -globalization, even technology over
time unless we do something so that everybody

00:49:11.410 --> 00:49:12.410
benefits.

00:49:12.410 --> 00:49:13.660
LIPSKY: The idea-but that's-part of that-but,
Nouriel, part of that skill bias stuff is

00:49:13.660 --> 00:49:18.440
the idea that technology favors the higher
skilled and not the lower skilled.

00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:19.440
BUITER: Some of it, actually.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:23.109
LIPSKY: But I thought technology did just
the opposite, right-made smart people-made

00:49:23.109 --> 00:49:25.620
stupid people smart, made weak people strong.

00:49:25.620 --> 00:49:32.500
So it's not obvious to me that the long-run
effect of advance-of accelerating technological

00:49:32.500 --> 00:49:39.240
advance is to disfavor or has had a regressive
distributional impact.

00:49:39.240 --> 00:49:41.250
I think we take that for granted without thinking
it through.

00:49:41.250 --> 00:49:43.650
BUITER: Well, I think just in general you're
right.

00:49:43.650 --> 00:49:51.619
But I think for artificial intelligence, and
you know, basically we'll be able to run banks

00:49:51.619 --> 00:49:53.490
without people before long, right?

00:49:53.490 --> 00:49:57.559
You know, hundreds of thousands of jobs that
are going to be wiped out here, replaced by

00:49:57.559 --> 00:50:02.460
just-you know, a few high-tech plants and
probably and high-tech-

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:04.140
LIPSKY: You're not turning Luddite, are you?

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:07.700
BUITER: Well, no, it's-and so-
ROUBINI: Only fine economic research within

00:50:07.700 --> 00:50:08.700
banks is destined.

00:50:08.700 --> 00:50:09.700
(Laughs.)

00:50:09.700 --> 00:50:12.220
BUITER: -and so I think-but, again, the challenge
will be, how do we deal with the world, which

00:50:12.220 --> 00:50:17.440
I think we are headed for, where basically
income and work will have to be decoupled?

00:50:17.440 --> 00:50:23.450
I think the answer is you move to a guaranteed
minimum income, what Tobin and Friedman called

00:50:23.450 --> 00:50:24.930
the negative income tax, right?

00:50:24.930 --> 00:50:29.559
The sort of super earned income tax credit,
but it's not even an earned income tax.

00:50:29.559 --> 00:50:32.030
The super income tax credit, even if you don't
do anything.

00:50:32.030 --> 00:50:34.410
MALLABY: An unearned income tax-
ROUBINI: Yeah, and then some.

00:50:34.410 --> 00:50:39.859
BUITER: And again, here the U.S. is, again,
poorly positioned because it has-doesn't have

00:50:39.859 --> 00:50:41.109
this leadership position.

00:50:41.109 --> 00:50:44.181
Even that doesn't solve the problem, because
there's a cultural problem that we have to

00:50:44.181 --> 00:50:47.349
learn to define ourselves as human beings
without work.

00:50:47.349 --> 00:50:50.980
And that is going to be much harder, even,
I think, introducing a guaranteed minimum

00:50:50.980 --> 00:50:51.980
income.

00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:55.069
LIPSKY: That we should all so live so long
to face this problem.

00:50:55.069 --> 00:50:56.069
(Laughter.)

00:50:56.069 --> 00:50:57.069
BUITER: I think that-I think that 50 years
from now-50 years from now.

00:50:57.069 --> 00:50:58.190
MALABY: We're getting a bit existential.

00:50:58.190 --> 00:51:01.190
I think we need-we need another question.

00:51:01.190 --> 00:51:02.480
Right there, yeah.

00:51:02.480 --> 00:51:03.480
LIPSKY: Yeah.

00:51:03.480 --> 00:51:04.480
(Chuckles.)

00:51:04.480 --> 00:51:05.480
Q: Thank you.

00:51:05.480 --> 00:51:06.480
Judith Kipper.

00:51:06.480 --> 00:51:07.480
(Coughs.)

00:51:07.480 --> 00:51:08.480
Excuse me.

00:51:08.480 --> 00:51:12.900
Climate change is probably the single most
important shared global problem that we have.

00:51:12.900 --> 00:51:18.970
We're already seeing its impact in all sorts
of ways in many places on the globe.

00:51:18.970 --> 00:51:22.920
It's had an economic impact in some countries
already.

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:29.230
Have you factored the consequences of climate
change into any of your assessments?

00:51:29.230 --> 00:51:36.500
And what do you think will be, in the next
10 (years), 20 (years), 30 years, those consequences?

00:51:36.500 --> 00:51:38.859
Because it seems to me it's going to be major.

00:51:38.859 --> 00:51:43.349
MALLABY: Well, let's focus that maybe on-well,
we could-we could take the example of the

00:51:43.349 --> 00:51:48.559
U.K. financial sector, which has been required
by the Bank of England, right, to change the

00:51:48.559 --> 00:51:51.790
way it accounts for contingent losses.

00:51:51.790 --> 00:51:57.119
BUITER: Yeah, and there's, again, also the-what
do you call it, the standard asset stuff,

00:51:57.119 --> 00:51:58.119
right?

00:51:58.119 --> 00:51:59.849
Yeah, yeah, it's a part of-yeah.

00:51:59.849 --> 00:52:00.849
ROUBINI: Right.

00:52:00.849 --> 00:52:05.240
Usually the argument on global climate change
is set in terms of at which point in time-10

00:52:05.240 --> 00:52:09.569
(years), 20 (years), 50 (years), 100 (years)
from now-Washington and New York are going

00:52:09.569 --> 00:52:10.760
to underwater and so on.

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:14.120
But I think the impact in the short term is
actually already evident.

00:52:14.120 --> 00:52:16.730
Take, for example, what's going on in the
Middle East.

00:52:16.730 --> 00:52:20.650
There are about 15 (million) to 20 million
people between Middle East, North Africa,

00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:22.990
and Sub-Saharan Africa that are displaced.

00:52:22.990 --> 00:52:24.720
And what are they displaced by?

00:52:24.720 --> 00:52:29.809
Global climate change that is leading to desertification,
desertification leads to collapse of water

00:52:29.809 --> 00:52:31.690
resources, and then your collapse of agriculture.

00:52:31.690 --> 00:52:32.740
And guess what?

00:52:32.740 --> 00:52:34.500
Then they start to kill each other.

00:52:34.500 --> 00:52:38.550
People forget that before the Syrian civil
war started, you had four years of a massive

00:52:38.550 --> 00:52:39.550
drought.

00:52:39.550 --> 00:52:41.750
And the same things happen in many of these
other failed states.

00:52:41.750 --> 00:52:46.589
So if Europe is worried about how to absorb
a million refugees last year, you have about

00:52:46.589 --> 00:52:48.880
20 million people that are knocking on the
door of Europe.

00:52:48.880 --> 00:52:52.930
And unless we do something about it, the next
few years are going to be a total disaster,

00:52:52.930 --> 00:52:57.000
economical also, in terms of what's going
to happen for Europe as a major threat.

00:52:57.000 --> 00:52:59.440
So the consequences of these things are not
50 years from now.

00:52:59.440 --> 00:53:00.990
They are things we have to think about today.

00:53:00.990 --> 00:53:02.250
BUITER: I agree.

00:53:02.250 --> 00:53:06.450
Sorry, you-
LIPSKY: I was going to say something very

00:53:06.450 --> 00:53:07.450
simple.

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:10.910
If we're not willing to talk about charges
for carbon use-i.e. carbon tax-then we're

00:53:10.910 --> 00:53:11.990
not serious.

00:53:11.990 --> 00:53:13.310
It's that simple.

00:53:13.310 --> 00:53:20.319
BUITER: First, I think it's sort of too late
no matter what we do, right, to stop us bouncing

00:53:20.319 --> 00:53:23.180
through the 2 percent-through 2-degree increase.

00:53:23.180 --> 00:53:27.120
So it's going to be a little show of horrors.

00:53:27.120 --> 00:53:34.319
And all we can do is, of course, try to cap
it as reasonably as possible, by carbon taxes

00:53:34.319 --> 00:53:41.310
most likely, and-but also then sort of resist
and respond, right?

00:53:41.310 --> 00:53:46.350
You want to definitely own stock in infrastructure
companies, right?

00:53:46.350 --> 00:53:53.520
In the Netherlands, we are raising already
the size of the dikes, the second Delta plan.

00:53:53.520 --> 00:53:57.690
We've created artificial dunes now also to
be ready for the global rising.

00:53:57.690 --> 00:54:05.099
We're going to need to construct 1,600 miles
of sea defenses in Bangladesh unless that

00:54:05.099 --> 00:54:07.250
country's going to be underwater before.

00:54:07.250 --> 00:54:11.680
And so-but Nouriel is completely right also
on the refugee issue.

00:54:11.680 --> 00:54:18.440
To call the Syrians and the other Middle Eastern,
or Central Asian, and Africans coming into

00:54:18.440 --> 00:54:24.040
Europe a refugee crisis as opposed to migration
crisis, that is incredibly superficial.

00:54:24.040 --> 00:54:31.920
Yes, the wars and the civil wars in the failed
states brought forward and probably speeded

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:33.520
up the arrival.

00:54:33.520 --> 00:54:41.780
But as he says-I actually have a number of
50 million between Africa, the Middle East,

00:54:41.780 --> 00:54:47.550
and Central Asia of countries where there's
demographics, right-an enormous number of

00:54:47.550 --> 00:54:49.280
young people.

00:54:49.280 --> 00:54:52.280
Second, climate change.

00:54:52.280 --> 00:54:57.569
And also, technical change on top of that-the
death, even independent of climate change,

00:54:57.569 --> 00:55:02.569
of labor-intensive agriculture in Africa,
the Middle East, and Central Asia.

00:55:02.569 --> 00:55:07.270
Then, fourth, terrible governance in almost
every country, right, in the region.

00:55:07.270 --> 00:55:10.040
And then two other little facts which also
are not going to go away.

00:55:10.040 --> 00:55:18.900
One, there has been enough real income growth
in even these poor countries to create a sizable

00:55:18.900 --> 00:55:23.040
number of people who have just enough income
to sort of pay the fare-pay the criminals

00:55:23.040 --> 00:55:25.210
to take them to Europe.

00:55:25.210 --> 00:55:27.230
And then, of course, they have the iPhones,
right?

00:55:27.230 --> 00:55:33.300
And so it's incredibly easy to plan the route
and to know, you know, where, you know, the

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:34.859
next roadblock is going to be.

00:55:34.859 --> 00:55:36.829
So Europe is going to have to face this, right?

00:55:36.829 --> 00:55:42.569
We're going to have 2 million every year for
the-for the foreseeable future.

00:55:42.569 --> 00:55:45.059
LIPSKY: So the simple answer to your question
is no.

00:55:45.059 --> 00:55:46.059
(Laughter.)

00:55:46.059 --> 00:55:47.770
MALLABY: Let's take another question.

00:55:47.770 --> 00:55:49.119
Yes, right there.

00:55:49.119 --> 00:55:50.119
Hi.

00:55:50.119 --> 00:55:52.530
The microphone is just coming behind you there.

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:53.530
Paula.

00:55:53.530 --> 00:55:54.900
Q: Hi, thank you.

00:55:54.900 --> 00:55:56.390
I'm Paula Stern.

00:55:56.390 --> 00:56:01.950
As Sebastian knows, usually I ask a question
that I think I know the answer to, but I'm

00:56:01.950 --> 00:56:05.319
really going to take a wild one on this one.

00:56:05.319 --> 00:56:12.030
You've talked about oil and you talked about
a carbon tax.

00:56:12.030 --> 00:56:17.410
You talked about our trade system-trading
system.

00:56:17.410 --> 00:56:26.089
As you know, energy-oil-has never been subject
to any of the trade rules.

00:56:26.089 --> 00:56:37.030
And I am wondering if you could imagine them
being subject-the trade in energy resources

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:38.829
being subject to the trade rules.

00:56:38.829 --> 00:56:45.790
Would it help in any way from a climate change
point of view, as well as an economic point

00:56:45.790 --> 00:56:47.480
of view in terms of growth?

00:56:47.480 --> 00:56:50.230
Can you image a world?

00:56:50.230 --> 00:56:58.780
And would it be better or not if we applied
the trade rules and restrict the kind of-which

00:56:58.780 --> 00:57:04.630
are based on economic principles-to the trade
in oil, or gas, and other resources?

00:57:04.630 --> 00:57:07.960
BUITER: What is question about, the oil trading-
LIPSKY: Yeah.

00:57:07.960 --> 00:57:10.609
MALLABY: What kind of trade rules do you mean,
Paula?

00:57:10.609 --> 00:57:13.220
Q: Well, the WTO.

00:57:13.220 --> 00:57:17.790
(Off mic)-on dumping, on-it just-I don't know.

00:57:17.790 --> 00:57:22.250
I mean, I don't-we've ever done it all these-all
these decades.

00:57:22.250 --> 00:57:23.250
BUITER: Is oil exempted from that?

00:57:23.250 --> 00:57:24.250
I didn't know that.

00:57:24.250 --> 00:57:25.920
Q: Even though it's the largest-traded item-
BUITER: Yeah?

00:57:25.920 --> 00:57:26.920
LIPSKY: Yeah.

00:57:26.920 --> 00:57:27.920
BUITER: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:57:27.920 --> 00:57:29.750
ROUBINI: I would make only one point.

00:57:29.750 --> 00:57:34.660
Probably compared to 20 (years), 30 (years),
40 years ago, OPEC is less powerful, and therefore

00:57:34.660 --> 00:57:40.931
its ability to affect through restricting
supply and so on global prices is less.

00:57:40.931 --> 00:57:43.670
First, it's very fragmented economically and
politically.

00:57:43.670 --> 00:57:46.710
One of the reasons why they're so-they're
not going to do a deal is because, of course,

00:57:46.710 --> 00:57:51.640
they don't want to make any favor to Iran
and Russia as long as Assad is in power in

00:57:51.640 --> 00:57:53.900
Syria, leaving aside other types of issues.

00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:57.780
And with the shale gas and oil revolution,
and lots of new production in other parts

00:57:57.780 --> 00:58:02.550
of the world outside of OPEC, probably, that
cartel is not as powerful as before.

00:58:02.550 --> 00:58:05.329
So its ability to affect price is not the
same as it was.

00:58:05.329 --> 00:58:10.980
You know, we had three major global stagflationary
shocks that were driven by geopolitical shock

00:58:10.980 --> 00:58:13.059
in the Middle East that led to an oil embargo.

00:58:13.059 --> 00:58:19.410
In 1973, Yom Kippur War between Israel and
Arab states; 1979, Iranian Revolution; 1990,

00:58:19.410 --> 00:58:20.500
Iraq invasion of Kuwait.

00:58:20.500 --> 00:58:22.990
This time around, the Middle East is burning.

00:58:22.990 --> 00:58:28.970
You have failed states right and left-Syria,
Lebanon, Iraq, Yemen, Libya, you name it.

00:58:28.970 --> 00:58:31.380
So you would think that the (fear of pain
?) would be through the roof.

00:58:31.380 --> 00:58:34.059
And instead, oil prices are lower and lower.

00:58:34.059 --> 00:58:35.059
Why?

00:58:35.059 --> 00:58:38.810
Because there is this glut of supply, in part
is the shale gas and oil revolution.

00:58:38.810 --> 00:58:42.130
So, luckily, the same kind of meltdown in
the Middle East that in the past led to spikes

00:58:42.130 --> 00:58:46.640
in oil prices today is not occurring, because
there are so many more suppliers.

00:58:46.640 --> 00:58:51.040
LIPSKY: But the-it's easy to be-to be either
pessimistic or cynical about this, but of

00:58:51.040 --> 00:58:56.700
course COP 21 meant that there are no major
countries that are in denial on climate change

00:58:56.700 --> 00:58:59.309
and the need for action.

00:58:59.309 --> 00:59:06.070
The decline-the decline in energy prices in
some countries like India, like Indonesia,

00:59:06.070 --> 00:59:10.410
have been used to reduce fossil-fuel subsidies.

00:59:10.410 --> 00:59:15.030
There is-there is room for practical action
in the near term, and you just got to keep

00:59:15.030 --> 00:59:16.200
the pressure up.

00:59:16.200 --> 00:59:17.410
It's not going to get solved quickly.

00:59:17.410 --> 00:59:21.829
But I think, ultimately, they're just-as I
said before, if we're not willing to impose

00:59:21.829 --> 00:59:23.920
a tax on carbon usage, then we're not serious.

00:59:23.920 --> 00:59:25.280
BUITER: You're not serious.

00:59:25.280 --> 00:59:26.280
I agree.

00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:27.280
Yeah-yeah, yeah.

00:59:27.280 --> 00:59:28.900
MALLABY: Let's squeeze one last question in.

00:59:28.900 --> 00:59:32.060
We'll go right over here, yeah.

00:59:32.060 --> 00:59:33.650
Q: Thanks, Sebastian.

00:59:33.650 --> 00:59:34.950
Frank Finelli from the Carlyle Group.

00:59:34.950 --> 00:59:37.990
Nouriel, I enjoyed your ECB trip report yesterday.

00:59:37.990 --> 00:59:42.170
You talked about the ECB acting, perhaps,
this summer, which is right about Brexit time.

00:59:42.170 --> 00:59:43.760
What do you think is going to happen with
Brexit?

00:59:43.760 --> 00:59:46.940
And how does that drive activity with the
ECB and the Fed?

00:59:46.940 --> 00:59:50.160
ROUBINI: Well, I-
MALLABY: We almost achieved an hour on the

00:59:50.160 --> 00:59:52.660
global economy without talking about Brexit,
so that's pretty good.

00:59:52.660 --> 00:59:55.150
ROUBINI: Well, we don't know what the result's
going to be.

00:59:55.150 --> 00:59:58.240
The vote is going to be close.

00:59:58.240 --> 01:00:01.630
If the U.K. remains, it remains.

01:00:01.630 --> 01:00:07.040
In the most interesting case, if there is
a Brexit vote, in that case I think the Europeans

01:00:07.040 --> 01:00:08.589
have two options.

01:00:08.589 --> 01:00:13.480
One is to say, well, can we renegotiate and
give you something more so that you're going

01:00:13.480 --> 01:00:15.060
to stay, and have another referendum?

01:00:15.060 --> 01:00:20.260
I spoke with some senior European officials
who told me-the French, the German, Italian-unless

01:00:20.260 --> 01:00:26.040
the vote is very, very close, if they decide
to exit, then we'll have to negotiate essentially

01:00:26.040 --> 01:00:30.190
a divorce, because otherwise Europe becomes
a Europe � la carte and the Poles and Hungarians,

01:00:30.190 --> 01:00:31.450
everybody else is going to say.

01:00:31.450 --> 01:00:35.140
And there are also limits to how much more
you can give them, given current treaty.

01:00:35.140 --> 01:00:37.230
So I'd say that option is not there.

01:00:37.230 --> 01:00:39.930
So there is-there is then negotiating of exit.

01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:44.549
I think the short-term impact is going to
be sell-off of the pound, sell-off of the

01:00:44.549 --> 01:00:48.611
stock market in the U.K., weakness in economic
activity because of confidence effect.

01:00:48.611 --> 01:00:55.960
The BoE most likely either stays on hold or
eases; unlikely they would hike, unless there

01:00:55.960 --> 01:00:59.250
is a disorderly collapse and a sudden stop.

01:00:59.250 --> 01:01:03.220
And then, in the medium term, I would say
the impact on the U.K. depends also on what

01:01:03.220 --> 01:01:04.220
happens in the rest of Europe.

01:01:04.220 --> 01:01:10.069
If the rest of Europe stays together as Europe
and the eurozone, then probably the impact

01:01:10.069 --> 01:01:14.080
is different than if there is a disorderly
collapse of the eurozone, in which case the

01:01:14.080 --> 01:01:20.040
U.K. might say we're better off being an island
outside of this messy club; they were sinking.

01:01:20.040 --> 01:01:23.290
On net, I would say the impact is going to
be negative over time.

01:01:23.290 --> 01:01:27.849
It depends very much on whether you can renegotiate
access to the single market.

01:01:27.849 --> 01:01:29.109
I don't think it's going to be easy.

01:01:29.109 --> 01:01:33.480
The Norwegian model implies you accept the
regulation as they are, and they have free

01:01:33.480 --> 01:01:34.480
mobility of labor.

01:01:34.480 --> 01:01:39.040
And even the Swiss model, where they renegotiated
for 40 years' access to the single market,

01:01:39.040 --> 01:01:40.540
the condition was free migration.

01:01:40.540 --> 01:01:44.191
So even if you could renegotiate � la carte
every agreement, at the end of the day EU

01:01:44.191 --> 01:01:48.829
requires free movement of labor as a condition
for access to the single market.

01:01:48.829 --> 01:01:52.130
And therefore, if you voted against it, you'll
have a problem-you'd have restricted access.

01:01:52.130 --> 01:01:58.660
I think the biggest loser is going to be probably
the city of London because, well, the French

01:01:58.660 --> 01:02:01.450
and the German(s) want the U.K. to stay in.

01:02:01.450 --> 01:02:05.680
If they really are so stupid to leave, they're
going to try, however hard that might be,

01:02:05.680 --> 01:02:11.030
to have Frankfurt and Paris as being more
important financial centers for Europe and

01:02:11.030 --> 01:02:13.660
try to crowd out as much as they can the city
of London.

01:02:13.660 --> 01:02:16.200
MALLABY: And I think we should probably leave
it there.

01:02:16.200 --> 01:02:17.760
Thank you to everyone.

01:02:17.760 --> 01:02:19.660
We've done-you know, we've contemplated the
end of money.

01:02:19.660 --> 01:02:22.620
We've contemplated existential angst in the
face of the end of work.

01:02:22.620 --> 01:02:23.620
(Laughter.)

01:02:23.620 --> 01:02:24.620
So thanks to the economists, and thank you
to you.

01:02:24.620 --> 01:02:25.620
(Laughter, applause.)

01:02:25.620 --> 01:02:26.620
LIPSKY: Thank you.

01:02:26.620 --> 01:02:27.620
BUITER: Thank you.

01:02:27.620 --> 01:02:27.621
(END)

